{"batchcomplete":"","query":{"normalized":[{"from":"Champion_Belt","to":"Champion Belt"},{"from":"Gremlin_Visage","to":"Gremlin Visage"},{"from":"Ninja_Scroll","to":"Ninja Scroll"},{"from":"Centennial_Puzzle","to":"Centennial Puzzle"},{"from":"Horn_Cleat","to":"Horn Cleat"},{"from":"Red_Mask","to":"Red Mask"},{"from":"Meat_on_the_Bone","to":"Meat on the Bone"},{"from":"Ink_Bottle","to":"Ink Bottle"},{"from":"Bag_of_Preparation","to":"Bag of Preparation"},{"from":"Mercury_Hourglass","to":"Mercury Hourglass"},{"from":"Ssserpent_Head","to":"Ssserpent Head"},{"from":"Thread_and_Needle","to":"Thread and Needle"},{"from":"Runic_Cube","to":"Runic Cube"},{"from":"Bottled_Tornado","to":"Bottled Tornado"},{"from":"Toxic_Egg","to":"Toxic Egg"},{"from":"Mutagenic_Strength","to":"Mutagenic Strength"}],"pages":{"2495":{"pageid":2495,"ns":0,"title":"Bag of Preparation","revisions":[{"revid":56890,"parentid":50804,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Bag of Preparation\n|Image       = BagofPreparation.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = At the start of each combat, draw 2 additional cards.\n|Flavor      = Oversized adventurer's pack. Has many pockets and straps.\n|GivesToPlayer = Draw\n}}\n{{R|Bag of Preparation}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Bag of Preparation}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Bag of Preparation}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Bag of Preparation}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Bag of Preparation}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Bag of Preparation}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Bag of Preparation}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Bag of Preparation}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Bag of Preparation}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Bag of Preparation}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Bag of Preparation}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Potentially gives more block with {{R|Cloak Clasp}} due to more cards drawn on the first turn of combat.\n* More cards can be discarded from the opening hand and re-drawn with {{R|Gambling Chip}}.\n* More cards are drawn to spend energy on during turn 1 with {{R|Lantern}} and {{R|Ancient Tea Set}}.\n* Potentially more attacks could be drawn on turn 1 to take advantage of {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} before its effect expires.\n* Essentially acts as having a second copy of {{R|Ring of the Snake}}, allowing for 9 cards to be drawn in the opening hand on turn 1.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* It's possible for the additional card draw of {{R|Bag of Preparation}} to be wasted if the player has already picked up other sources of card draw that affect turn 1 (such as {{R|Ring of the Snake}} or {{R|Snecko Eye}}) or effects that can add more cards to the starting hand (such as {{R|Ninja Scroll}} and {{R|Holy Water}}, or drawing {{C|Deus Ex Machina}} and {{C|Endless Agony}}).\n* If {{R|Mark of Pain}} is acquired ''before'' {{R|Bag of Preparation}}, it's possible for {{R|Bag of Preparation}} to draw one or more of the {{C|Wound|Wounds}} that get added to the player's draw pile.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The backpack shown in the card art for {{C|Prepared}} looks to be a {{R|Bag of Preparation}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Fixed Bag of Preparation not drawing cards affected by Confusion power.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Updated relic art for Bag of Preparation\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2552":{"pageid":2552,"ns":0,"title":"Bottled Tornado","revisions":[{"revid":56911,"parentid":50839,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Bottled Tornado\n|Image       = BottledTornado.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Upon pickup, choose a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} card. <br>At the start of each combat, this card will be in your hand.\n|Flavor      = The bottle gently hums and whirs.\n}}\n{{R|Bottled Tornado}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Bottled Tornado}}.\n* Can be obtained from the {{E|Dead Adventurer}} event by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Bottled Tornado}}).\n* Can be obtained from the {{E|Wheel of Change}} event by earning {{R|Bottled Tornado}} as a prize.\n* Can be obtained from {{E|The Colosseum}} event by choosing the {{Frame|VICTORY!}} option and getting {{R|Bottled Tornado}} as the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic reward.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Cannot be obtained from {{R|Calling Bell}} and \"Obtain Relic\" events, that drop the relic without it being a combat reward. This means, for example, {{R|Bottled Tornado}} can be obtained from {{E|Dead Adventurer}} if the [[Elite]] is beaten, but cannot be obtained if it is found from the {{Frame|Search}} option.\n* Can only be obtained if your deck has a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\nBottling a card is '''not''' the same as \"making it {{KW|Innate}}\", as there are a few quirks that only affect bottled cards:\n* A bottled card can't be removed from the player's deck.\n* A bottled card can still be [[Transform|transformed]] as normal. However, this will also un-bottle it and it will no longer be drawn in the opening hand. This effectively disables {{R|Bottled Tornado}}.\n* A bottled card can be duplicated from effects such as {{R|Dolly's Mirror}} and {{E|Duplicator}}, but the duplicate will not be bottled.\n* A bottled card will still have its cost randomized by {{R|Snecko Eye}}.\n\n=== Synergies ===\nWhile most {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} benefit from being put in play as soon as possible, there are many considerations to make when multiple good options are available.\n* Bottling defensive {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} can be useful for decks that lack the draw and/or energy to reliably mitigate damage in the early turns (such as {{C|Feel No Pain}}, {{C|Footwork}}, {{C|Buffer}}, and {{C|Like Water}}).\n* Bottling {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} that aid in drawing cards can be useful for decks that otherwise lack the draw to reliably put the Power in play in the early turns (such as {{C|Dark Embrace}}, {{C|Tools of the Trade}}, {{C|Heatsinks}}, or {{C|Rushdown}}).\n* Bottling {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} that aid in setting up scaling can be useful for long fights (such as [[Bosses]]) and are often uniquely well-suited to being bottled so that their effects have the maximum possible time to provide scaling (such as {{C|Demon Form}}, {{C|Noxious Fumes}}, {{C|Echo Form}}, and {{C|Deva Form}}).\n* Relics such as {{R|Lantern}} and {{R|Mummified Hand}} can help smooth out the {{KW|Energy}} requirements that often make {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} difficult to play as some of them provide little or no benefit on the turn they're played.\n\n== Bugs ==\n* If a card is [[Transform|transformed]] (which will un-bottle it), {{R|Bottled Tornado}}'s description will still read that the original card will start in the player's hand (even though it won't as the card doesn't exist anymore).\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Bottled Tornado}} may be inspired by a popular science experiment that's often used to teach children about [https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centripetal_force centripetal force].<ref>https://coolscienceexperimentshq.com/tornado-in-a-bottle/</ref>\n* {{R|Bottled Tornado}} is one of relics in the \"bottle relic set\" which all allow the player to choose a card of a different type to start in their opening hand; the others being {{R|Bottled Flame}} for {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} and {{R|Bottled Lightning}} for {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}}.\n* {{R|Bottled Tornado}} is the only bottle relic where no character starts with its card type in their starting deck.\n* Since every {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} card is either {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} or {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}}, it is ''slightly'' less likely to encounter {{R|Bottled Tornado}} compared to {{R|Bottled Flame}} or {{R|Bottled Lightning}} as each character has many {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Common&type:Attack|Common Attacks}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Common&type:Skill|Common Skills}} they can add to their deck and is therefore easier to fulfill the [[#Spawn Restrictions|spawn restriction]] requirements of those bottles.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Bottled Cards upgraded from Mind Bloom event have their description updated correctly.\n* '''Weekly Patch 49: The Designer'''\n** Wheel of Change event no longer makes you stuck if the only removable cards in your deck are bottled.\n* '''Weekly Patch 46: Adrenaline'''\n** Purchasing Bottle relics after removing the last possible card to bottle out of your deck will no longer softlock.\n* '''Weekly Patch 30: Skewer'''\n** Peace Pipe relic cannot get rid of Bottled cards anymore.\n* '''Weekly Patch 27: Hello World'''\n** Fix for Bottled cards showing up in Wheel of Change event's Purge result.\n* '''Weekly Patch 21: Channel Lightning'''\n** Fixed crash when travelling to a new room while a Bottle relic's screen was open.\n* '''Weekly Patch 13: Thinking Ahead'''\n** Bottled Flame, Lightning, and Tornado will no longer spawn if there are no valid cards.\n* '''Weekly Patch 8: Ooh Shiny!'''\n** Bottled Tornado relic added.\n** Polished art for Bottled Flame, Lightning, and Tornado relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!'''\n** Potential Fix for an edge case Bottled Tornado/Lightning issue.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2489":{"pageid":2489,"ns":0,"title":"Centennial Puzzle","revisions":[{"revid":56930,"parentid":55684,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Centennial Puzzle\n|Image       = CentennialPuzzle.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = The first time you lose HP each combat, draw 3 cards.\n|Flavor      = Upon solving the puzzle you feel a powerful warmth in your chest.\n|GivesToPlayer = Draw\n|InteractsWith = Lose HP\n}}\n{{R|Centennial Puzzle}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Centennial Puzzle}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Centennial Puzzle}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Centennial Puzzle}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n{{R|Centennial Puzzle}} only triggers when ''HP is lost'', not when ''taking damage''. Receiving damage that is fully {{KW|Block|Blocked}} will not trigger the relic.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Cards that specifically state they cause the player to ''lose HP'' can trigger {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} through {{KW|Block}}. These effects are:\n** {{C|Brutality}}\n** {{C|Hemokinesis}}\n** {{C|Bloodletting}}\n** {{C|Combust}}* (not really a synergy; the cards will be immediately discarded since this is an end-of-turn effect)\n** {{C|Offering}}\n** {{C|J.A.X.}}\n** {{C|Pain}}\n** {{C|Regret}}* (not really a synergy; the cards will be immediately discarded since this is an end-of-turn effect)\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} can be triggered by attacking into an enemy with {{BD|Thorns}} or {{BD|Sharp Hide}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* End-of-turn damage effects (such as {{C|Combust}} and {{C|Regret}}) will trigger {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} and immediately discard its drawn cards with the rest of the hand.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} is a reference and spoof of the Millennium Puzzle from Yu-Gi-Oh.<ref>https://yugipedia.com/wiki/Millennium_Puzzle</ref>\n** The root word of ''Centennial'' is ''century'', meaning 100 years. ''Millennium'' means 1000 years.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 35: Aggregate'''\n** Centennial Puzzle no longer draws cards when taking 0 damage as a result of things like Buffer.\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Centennial Puzzle relic is now common rarity.\n* '''Weekly Patch 9: Ascension'''\n** Fixed bug where Centennial Puzzle doesn't trigger off of enemy thorns.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1443":{"pageid":1443,"ns":0,"title":"Champion Belt","revisions":[{"revid":56329,"parentid":55687,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Champion Belt\n| Image       = ChampionBelt.png\n| Rarity      = Rare\n| Character   = Ironclad\n| Description = Whenever you apply {{BD|Vulnerable}}, also apply 1 {{BD|Weak}}.\n| Flavor      = Only the greatest may wear this belt.\n| GivesToEnemy = Weak\n| InteractsWith = Vulnerable\n}} \n{{Power Infobox|Vulnerable}} {{Power Infobox|Weak}}\n{{R|Champion Belt}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Ironclad|Ironclad-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Champion Belt}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Champion Belt}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Champion Belt}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Champion Belt}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Champion Belt}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Champion Belt}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Champion Belt}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Champion Belt}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Champion Belt}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Champion Belt}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Champion Belt}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Champion Belt}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained by [[Ironclad]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Cards ===\n* {{C|Thunderclap}}, {{C|Shockwave}}, and the {{KW|Upgrade|upgraded}} {{C|Trip}}+ will apply 1 {{BD|Weak}} to all enemies, which can be an effective form of damage mitigation when multiple enemies are attacking.\n* {{C|Bash}}, {{C|Uppercut}}, and {{C|Trip}} will apply 1 {{BD|Weak}} to the targeted enemy.\n* Has no interaction with {{C|Berserk}}.\n\n=== Relics ===\n* {{R|Paper Phrog}} increases the effectiveness of {{BD|Vulnerable}} to a 75% damage bonus.\n* {{R|Hand Drill}} can apply {{BD|Weak}} to an enemy any time their {{KW|Block}} is broken.\n* {{R|Bag of Marbles}} will apply 1 {{BD|Vulnerable}} and 1 {{BD|Weak}} to all enemies at the start of combat.\n\n=== Potions ===\n* {{P|Fear Potion}} will apply 1 {{BD|Weak}} to the targeted enemy.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Champion Belt}} is owned by {{M|The Champ}}. Fighting him while holding it causes him to say \"{{TS||shake|THAT'S MY BELT!!}}\" on his first turn.\n** This is a subtle hint to the fact that he is really angry.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Champ boss now recognizes if you have his belt.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3071":{"pageid":3071,"ns":0,"title":"Ginger","revisions":[{"revid":56127,"parentid":55799,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Ginger\n|Image       = Ginger.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = You can no longer become {{BD|Weak|Weakened}}.\n|Flavor      = A potent tool in many tonics.\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Weak}}{{R|Ginger}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Ginger}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Ginger}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Ginger}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Ginger}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Ginger}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Ginger}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Ginger}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Ginger}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Ginger}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Ginger}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Ginger}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Ginger}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Ginger}} will trigger before {{BD|Artifact}} whenever {{BD|Weak}} would be applied. Or in other words, no {{BD|Artifact}} will be consumed if the player would receive {{BD|Weak}} while possessing {{R|Ginger}}.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Ginger}} can help decks that heavily rely on playing {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} to deal damage against enemies that can apply {{BD|Weak}}.\n* {{R|Ginger}} will block the {{BD|Weak}} applied by {{R|Gremlin Visage}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Ginger is the root of the ''Zingiber officinale'' plant. It is often used in many traditional medicines for nausea and stomach aches, and as a spice in many cuisines worldwide.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger</ref>\n** The effect of {{R|Ginger}} is likely inspired from its real-world medicinal applications throughout history. This is also referenced in its flavor text.\n* {{R|Ginger}} is one of the relics in the \"edible relic set\" that all reduce [[Debuffs|debuffs]]; the others being {{R|Turnip}} for blocking {{BD|Frail}} and {{R|Odd Mushroom}} for lessening the effectiveness of {{BD|Vulnerable}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* Weekly Patch 50: The Final Act\n** Turnip and Ginger relics now prevent Artifact from triggering when they nullify debuffs.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3126":{"pageid":3126,"ns":0,"title":"Gremlin Visage","revisions":[{"revid":55808,"parentid":51009,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Gremlin Visage\n|Image       = GremlinMask.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = Face Trader\n|Description = Start each combat with 1 {{BD|Weak}}.\n|Flavor      = Time to run.\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Weak}}{{R|Gremlin Visage}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained from {{E|Face Trader}} by selecting the {{Frame|Trade}} option and randomly receiving {{R|Gremlin Visage}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}'s {{BD|Artifact}} will prevent {{R|Gremlin Visage}}'s {{BD|Weak}} regardless of which order they were acquired in.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Gremlin Visage}} can be traded for {{R|N'loth's Gift}} if it's randomly chosen in the {{E|N'loth}} [[Event]]. This will prevent the player from becoming {{BD|Weak}} at the start of each combat.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Internally called <code>GremlinMask</code>.\n* {{R|Gremlin Visage}} is the face of some kind of [[Gremlins|Gremlin]] that was taken by the {{E|Face Trader}}.\n** {{R|Gremlin Visage}} is the only mask given by {{E|Face Trader}} that doesn't directly relate to a specific enemy or NPC.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** Face Trader event will no longer give out duplicate relics on Endless mode.\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Adding event relic Gremlin Visage.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2570":{"pageid":2570,"ns":0,"title":"Horn Cleat","revisions":[{"revid":50844,"parentid":49471,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Horn Cleat\n|Image       = HornCleat.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = At the start of your 2nd turn, gain 14 {{KW|Block}}.\n|Flavor      = Pleasant to hold in the hand. What was it for?\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Block}}{{R|Horn Cleat}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Horn Cleat}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Horn Cleat}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Horn Cleat}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Horn Cleat}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Horn Cleat}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Horn Cleat}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Horn Cleat}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Horn Cleat}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Horn Cleat}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Horn Cleat}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Horn Cleat}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Some enemies are guaranteed to never attack on their first turn (such as {{M|Gremlin Nob}}), or have more devastating attacks on turn 2 (such as {{M|Reptomancer}} with their summoned {{M|Dagger|Daggers}}). {{R|Horn Cleat}} can be effective at assisting in damage mitigation for these fights with dangerous second turns.\n* The {{KW|Block}} can help delay the activation of {{BD|Buffer}} gained through {{R|Fossilized Helix}} or {{C|Buffer}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Horn Cleat}} makes it more difficult to trigger {{R|Centennial Puzzle}}, should you want to (especially against enemies that don't attack on turn 1).\n* The {{KW|Block}} is wasted on enemies that do no damage on the second turn, unless the '''block''' is carried over to the next turn using effects like {{C|Barricade}}, {{C|Blur}}, and {{R|Calipers}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Horn Cleat}} is the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic in a set of relics which all grant {{KW|Block}} on different turns of combat; the others being the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} {{R|Anchor}} on turn 1 and the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} {{R|Captain's Wheel}} on turn 3.\n* A horn cleat is a nautical device used for securing rope. They can be seen on the outer railings of many ships and docksides.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleat_(nautical)</ref>\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2370":{"pageid":2370,"ns":0,"title":"Ink Bottle","revisions":[{"revid":50845,"parentid":37184,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Ink Bottle\n|Image       = InkBottle.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Whenever you play 10 cards, draw 1 card.\n|Flavor      = Once exhausted, appears to refill itself in a different color.\n|Keywords    = Draw\n}}\n{{R|Ink Bottle}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]] that draws 1 card for every 10 cards played. The counter that keeps track of how many cards have been played is not reset between combats.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Ink Bottle}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Ink Bottle}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Ink Bottle}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Ink Bottle}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Ink Bottle}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Ink Bottle}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Ink Bottle}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Ink Bottle}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Ink Bottle}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Ink Bottle}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Ink Bottle}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Ink Bottle}}'s counter increments ''after'' the played card's effect is finished resolving. For example, the card draw will be wasted if the 10th card played is {{C|Scrawl}} as the player's hand will already be full when {{R|Ink Bottle}} triggers.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Effects that allow a card to be played multiple times (such as {{C|Double Tap}}, {{C|Burst}}, {{C|Echo Form}}, and {{P|Duplication Potion}}) will increment {{R|Ink Bottle}}'s counter for the extra card played as well, effectively granting a total +2 to the counter.\n* Effects that allow multiple cheap cards to be played (such as {{C|Blade Dance}} and {{C|All for One}}) can be used to quickly stack up {{R|Ink Bottle}}'s counter.\n* {{R|Ninja Scroll}} allows for an easy +3 to {{R|Ink Bottle}}'s counter at the start of every combat.\n* There is a (very rare) infinite card draw combo that can be performed with {{R|Ink Bottle}}, {{C|Necronomicurse}}, {{R|Blue Candle}}, and {{R|Tungsten Rod}}.\n** The {{C|Necronomicurse}} can be repeatedly {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausted}} with {{R|Blue Candle}} (which counts as +1 card played for {{R|Ink Bottle}}'s counter) and {{R|Tungsten Rod}} prevents any HP from being lost.\n** When combined with sufficient repeatable {{KW|Energy}} generation (such as enough copies of {{C|Concentrate}} or {{C|TURBO}}, or a small deck combined with {{R|Sundial}}), this can allow the player to infinitely draw and play every card in their deck until they win the fight.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2590":{"pageid":2590,"ns":0,"title":"Meat on the Bone","revisions":[{"revid":55855,"parentid":55461,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Meat on the Bone\n|Image       = MeatontheBone.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = If your HP is at or below 50% at the end of combat, heal 12 HP.\n|Flavor      = The meat keeps replenishing, never seeming to fully run out.\n}}\n{{R|Meat on the Bone}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Meat on the Bone}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Meat on the Bone}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Meat on the Bone}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Meat on the Bone}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Meat on the Bone}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Meat on the Bone}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Meat on the Bone}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Meat on the Bone}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Meat on the Bone}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Meat on the Bone}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Meat on the Bone}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Meat on the Bone}}'s healing occurs at the end of combat but before the rewards screen appears. This allows it to benefit from the healing bonus from {{R|Magic Flower}}, healing 18 HP instead of 12 HP.\n* {{R|Meat on the Bone}}'s healing can be particularly useful when the player's Max HP has been massively reduced by various events with devastating Max HP penalties (such as {{E|Council of Ghosts}} or {{E|Vampires}}).\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* As with all healing effects, {{R|Meat on the Bone}} restores no HP if the player possesses {{R|Mark of the Bloom}}.\n* Having both {{R|Meat on the Bone}} and {{R|Red Skull}} can make it more difficult to remain at a level of HP that allows {{R|Red Skull}} to provide {{BD|Strength}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The visual design of {{R|Meat on the Bone}} is a common depiction of a chunk of meat on a femur bone; a popular stylistic choice in cartoon-style drawings.<ref>https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/CartoonMeat</ref> It is unlikely to be a direct reference to any one specific pop culture property due to the ubiquity of its design.<ref>Could be disproven with a quote from a developer and a citation/link for proof of confirmation.</ref>\n** Examples include [https://pm1.aminoapps.com/7758/fb333e893bbf2a37ebebb97494e8a9c69f0655c6r1-640-360v2_hq.jpg Danganronpa] (where it's used as a murder weapon in the game, and became a popular meme in the community), [https://www.polygon.com/22797421/one-piece-luffys-meat-on-the-bone-recipe/ One Piece] (where the main character is often motivated by eating meat), [https://monsterhunterworld.wiki.fextralife.com/Raw+Meat Monster Hunter] (where it's a consumable item obtained from many different monsters), and the [https://emojipedia.org/meat-on-bone#technical \"Meat on Bone\" Unicode code point] <code>U+1F356</code> (\ud83c\udf56).\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 6: We're Back!'''\n** Black Blood, Burning Blood, and Meat on the Bone no longer heal you if you are dead.\n* '''Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!'''\n** Meat on the Bone, Red Skull, Slime's Split, and Berserk all trigger on less than or equal to 50% HP and desc are updated to reflect this\n* '''Weekly Update 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** Meat on the Bone relic buff. (9 -> 12 HP)\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2318":{"pageid":2318,"ns":0,"title":"Mercury Hourglass","revisions":[{"revid":55858,"parentid":50850,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Mercury Hourglass\n|Image       = MercuryHourglass.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = At the start of your turn, deal 3 damage to ALL enemies.\n|Flavor      = An enchanted hourglass that endlessly drips.\n}}\n{{R|Mercury Hourglass}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]]. At the start of each turn in combat, it deals 3 damage to all enemies. This damage is not treated as {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} damage, and therefore does not benefit from effects like {{BD|Strength}}, {{BD|Vulnerable}}, or {{R|The Boot}}.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Mercury Hourglass}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Mercury Hourglass}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Mercury Hourglass}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Mercury Hourglass}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} triggers before the player has drawn their hand for the turn, except on the first turn of combat where it only triggers ''after'' the opening hand has been drawn.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} can provide a reliable source of damage for decks that otherwise lack effective ways to deal with lots of small enemies, or must frequently play turns focusing on defence and not dealing damage, or that favor long drawn-out fights.\n* Since {{R|Mercury Hourglass}}' damage is not {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} damage, it will not cause the player to take damage from {{BD|Thorns}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Mercury Hourglass}}' damage will not change {{M|Writhing Mass}}' intent.\n* {{R|Mercury Hourglass}}' damage may make it easier to wake up {{M|Lagavulin}} by removing some of its {{BD|Metallicize}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Mercury Hourglass}} may be a reference to the \"unofficial\" Yu-Gi-Oh card by the same name. The Mercury Hourglass card first appears in the 45th episode of ''Yu-Gi-Oh! GX'' and is not a real card that can be acquired in the physical Yu-Gi-Oh trading card game.<ref>https://yugipedia.com/wiki/Mercury_Hourglass</ref>\n* Mercury is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature (with a melting point of roughly -39 \u00b0C or roughly -38 \u00b0F).<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury_(element)</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 2: Slimes!'''\n** Mercury Hourglass description updated for clarity.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3140":{"pageid":3140,"ns":0,"title":"Mutagenic Strength","revisions":[{"revid":55866,"parentid":51024,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Mutagenic Strength\n|Image       = MutagenicStrength.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = Augmenter\n|Description = Start each combat with 3 {{BD|Strength}}. At the end of your first turn, lose 3 {{BD|Strength}}.\n|Flavor      = \"The results seem fleeting, triggering when the subject is in danger.\" - Unknown\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Strength}} {{Power Infobox|Strength Down}}\n{{R|Mutagenic Strength}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]]. It grants 3 {{BD|Strength}} and 3 {{BD|Strength Down}} at the start of combat.\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained from {{E|Augmenter}} by selecting the {{Frame|Ingest Mutagens}} option.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* When calculating damage dealt, all the additive bonuses are added first, and then multiplicative bonuses are multiplied. The final result is rounded down. {{R|Mutagenic Strength}}'s {{BD|Strength}} is an additive bonus.\n<!-- If you decide to change this section on Clockwork Souvenir in some way, please also change it in the Clockwork Souvenir page for consistency! -->\n* If the player has both {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} and {{R|Mutagenic Strength}}, the effect is dependent on the order in which the relics were obtained. The order of the effects is also somewhat counter-intuitive, and can seem \"backwards\" of their pickup order.\n** If {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} was obtained first, {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} applies its effects before {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}. The player starts each combat with 3 {{BD|Strength Down}}, 3 {{BD|Strength}}, and 1 {{BD|Artifact}}.\n** If {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} was obtained first, the {{BD|Artifact}} from {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}} is applied first and negates the {{BD|Strength Down}} from {{R|Mutagenic Strength}}. The player starts each combat with 3 {{BD|Strength}}, which will remain for the rest of the fight.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Drawing more cards on turn 1 from relics such as {{R|Bag of Preparation}} and {{R|Snecko Eye}} can make it easier to use the {{BD|Strength}} before it wears off.\n* {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} that have {{KW|Innate}} (such as {{C|Backstab}} and {{C|Dramatic Entrance}}) or that are added to the player's opening hand via {{R|Bottled Flame}} can consistently take advantage of the temporary {{BD|Strength}}.\n* As with all {{BD|Strength}} effects, {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} that hit multiple times (such as {{C|Pummel}}, {{C|Riddle with Holes}}, {{C|Barrage}}, and {{C|Tantrum}}) gain a greater benefit from {{BD|Strength}}.\n* {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}, {{P|Ancient Potion}}, and {{R|Orange Pellets}} can be used to remove the {{BD|Strength Down}}, allowing the player to keep the 3 {{BD|Strength}} for the entire combat.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* If the player has a more important [[Debuff]] they want to prevent with {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}'s {{BD|Artifact}} (such as {{BD|Wraith Form}} or {{BD|Bias}}), it can be much harder to do so if they also possess {{R|Mutagenic Strength}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** Localization: Mutagenic Strength, Meal Ticket, and Abacus now support more flexible descriptions.\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** rt added for Mutagenic Strength relic.\n** Augmenter event will no longer give out duplicate relics on Endless mode.\n* '''Weekly Patch 39: We Meet Again!'''\n** Hotfix: Fixed issue where Mutagenic Strength relic was beta only.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1804":{"pageid":1804,"ns":0,"title":"Ninja Scroll","revisions":[{"revid":56371,"parentid":50865,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Ninja Scroll\n| Image       = NinjaScroll.png\n| Rarity      = Uncommon\n| Character   = Silent\n| Description = At the start each combat, add 3 {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} into your hand.\n| Flavor      = Contains the secrets of assassination.\n}}\n{{Small Card_Infobox|Shiv}}\n{{R|Ninja Scroll}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Silent|Silent-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]] that adds three {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} to the player's hand immediately at the start of each combat.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Ninja Scroll}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Ninja Scroll}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Ninja Scroll}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Ninja Scroll}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Ninja Scroll}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Ninja Scroll}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Ninja Scroll}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Ninja Scroll}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Ninja Scroll}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Ninja Scroll}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Ninja Scroll}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Ninja Scroll}} will always trigger ''before'' the player draws any cards for their opening hand. This includes the normal 5 cards drawn per turn, and card draw effects such as {{R|Ring of the Snake}} and {{R|Bag of Preparation}}.\n* {{R|Ninja Scroll}} will trigger with other \"before opening hand\" relics (such as {{R|Toolbox}}) in pickup order (i.e. from left to right in the player's relic bar).\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{C|Calculated Gamble}} and {{R|Gambling Chip}} can be used to discard one or more of the {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} to draw additional cards.\n* {{R|Kunai}}, {{R|Ornamental Fan}}, and {{R|Shuriken}} can all be activated on turn 1.\n* If {{C|After Image}} is in the opening hand (whether by upgrading it to gain {{KW|Innate}} or using {{R|Bottled Tornado}}), it can grant a total of 3 {{KW|Block}} when playing the {{C|Shiv|Shivs}}.\n* The {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} provide an easy and reliable +3 to the counters of {{R|Pen Nib}}, {{R|Nunchaku}}, and {{R|Ink Bottle}}. This is especially useful since their counters keep their value between combats.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Dead Branch}} will create three new cards in your hand when the {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} {{KW|Exhaust}}.\n\n=== Negative ===\n* Since the {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} are added to the hand before any cards are drawn, it becomes much easier to waste additional card draw with relics such as {{R|Ring of the Snake}} and {{R|Bag of Preparation}}.\n* {{R|Velvet Choker}} limits the player to playing 6 cards per turn, potentially making the {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} or other cards in their hand unplayable.\n* {{R|Strange Spoon}} may discard the {{C|Shiv|Shivs}}, causing the player to redraw them on a future turn.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** Effects that spawn cards in your hand at start of turn like Infinite Blades and Ninja Scroll will now trigger before card draw.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2527":{"pageid":2527,"ns":0,"title":"Orichalcum","revisions":[{"revid":55880,"parentid":50818,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Orichalcum\n|Image       = Orichalcum.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = If you end your turn without {{KW|Block}}, gain 6 {{KW|Block}}.\n|Flavor      = A green tinted metal of an unknown origin. Seemingly indestructible.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Block}}{{R|Orichalcum}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Orichalcum}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Orichalcum}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Orichalcum}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Orichalcum}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Orichalcum}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Orichalcum}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Orichalcum}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Orichalcum}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Orichalcum}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Orichalcum}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\nThe following effects will trigger ''after'' {{R|Orichalcum}} has already checked if the player has no {{KW|Block}} (i.e. they can be used effectively alongside {{R|Orichalcum}} without preventing its {{KW|Block}} gain):\n* {{BD|Metallicize}}\n* {{BD|Plated Armor}}\n* {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}}\n* {{R|Cloak Clasp}}\n* {{KW|Ethereal}} cards that {{KW|Exhaust}} at the end of the turn and grant {{KW|Block}} due to {{C|Feel No Pain}}\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Orichalcum}} can still provide {{KW|Block}} even if the player is affected by the {{BD|No Block}} effect from {{C|Panic Button}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{C|After Image}} will easily disable {{R|Orichalcum}} as playing even 1 card will disable the relic's {{KW|Block}} gain for that turn.\n* Effects that provide {{KW|Block}} at the start of the player's turn (such as {{R|Self-Forming Clay}} and {{C|Dodge and Roll}}) will disable {{R|Orichalcum}} unless the player removes the {{KW|Block}} before the end of their turn (such as by {{BD|Beat of Death}}).\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Orichalcum is a metal mentioned in several ancient writings, including the story of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantis Atlantis] in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critias_(dialogue) ''Critias''] of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plato Plato].<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orichalcum</ref>\n** Ingots of orichalcum have been found off the coast of Gela in Sicily and analyzed to be a specific alloy consisting of mostly copper, a lesser amount of zinc, and small percentages of nickel, lead, and iron.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orichalcum#Ingots_found</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Updated relic art for Orichalcum.\n* '''Weekly Patch 23: Testing Continues...'''\n** Orichalcum now triggers even if Frost orbs trigger. ...\n* '''Weekly Patch 21: Channel Lightning'''\n** Fixed bug where Orichalcum relic never stopped flashing.\n* '''Weekly Patch 9: Ascension'''\n** Fixed issue where Orichalcum will start flashing even if you have Block from Barricade/Blur/Calipers.\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** Orichalcum gets a very minor buff. ''(!!Unconfirmed!! buffed from 5 block -> 6 block)''\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3131":{"pageid":3131,"ns":0,"title":"Red Mask","revisions":[{"revid":55917,"parentid":51015,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}} \n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Red Mask\n|Image       = RedMask.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = Masked Bandits, Tomb of Lord Red Mas\n|Description = At the start of each combat, apply 1 {{BD|Weak}} to ALL enemies.\n|Flavor      = This very stylish looking mask belongs to the leader of the Red Mask Bandits. Technically that makes you the leader now?\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Weak}}\n{{R|Red Mask}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from {{E|Masked Bandits}} by selecting the {{Frame|Fight}} option and defeating the [[Masked Bandits]].\n* Can be obtained from {{E|Tomb of Lord Red Mask}} by selecting the {{Frame|Offer X Gold}} option, where X is equal to all of the player's [[Gold]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* When calculating damage dealt, all the additive bonuses are added first, and then multiplicative bonuses are multiplied. The final result is rounded down. {{R|Red Mask}}'s {{BD|Weak}} is a multiplicative bonus.\n* {{R|Red Mask}} will trigger with other \"after opening hand\" relics (such as {{R|Bag of Marbles}} and {{R|Twisted Funnel}}) in pickup order (i.e. from left to right in the player's relic bar).\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Paper Krane}} will improve the effectiveness of the {{BD|Weak}} applied by {{R|Red Mask}}.\n* Applying {{BD|Weak}} at the start of combat is an effective way to lower the pressure of getting attacked on the first turn of combat when the player has had the least amount of time to set up for the fight.\n* If the player has already acquired {{R|Red Mask}} from the {{E|Masked Bandits}} [[Event]] in [[Act 2]], it can be used to receive 222 [[Gold]] in the {{E|Tomb of Lord Red Mask}} [[Event]] in [[Act 3]] by selecting the {{Frame|Don the Red Mask}} option. The player still gets to keep the {{R|Red Mask}} relic even if they do this.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* Removes 1 {{BD|Artifact}} from enemies that start combat with some applied, such as the {{M|Sentry|Sentries}} or {{M|Spheric Guardian}}.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1448":{"pageid":1448,"ns":0,"title":"Runic Cube","revisions":[{"revid":56331,"parentid":55926,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Runic Cube\n| Image       = RunicCube.png\n| Rarity      = Boss\n| Character   = Ironclad\n| Description = Whenever you lose HP, draw 1 card.\n| Flavor      = The runes are indecipherable.\n| GivesToPlayer = Draw\n| InteractsWith = Lose HP\n}}\n{{R|Runic Cube}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Ironclad|Ironclad-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. Whenever the player loses any amount of HP for any reason during combat, they will draw one card.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Runic Cube}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Ironclad]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Runic Cube}} triggers immediately after the player has lost HP. For example, {{R|Runic Cube}} will draw 1 card from {{C|Offering}}'s damage before {{C|Offering}}'s own card draw takes effect.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Runic Cube}} triggers on ''all'' HP loss effects, including instances where it is self-inflicted. The following effects can allow the player to draw cards during their own turn through self-inflicted HP loss:\n** {{C|Bloodletting}}\n** {{C|Combust}} (although the card will be immediately discarded)\n** {{C|Hemokinesis}}\n** {{C|Brutality}}\n** {{C|Offering}}\n** {{C|J.A.X.}}\n** {{C|Pain}}\n** {{C|Regret}} (although the card will be immediately discarded)\n** {{R|Blue Candle}} (requires a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} to be in the player's hand)\n*** {{C|Necronomicurse}} can allow the player to repeatedly {{KW|Exhaust}} it with {{R|Blue Candle}} to draw as many cards as they're willing to take damage for.\n* {{C|Pain}} can be a surprisingly effective form of card draw when combined with {{R|Runic Cube}}, allowing every card played while it's in hand to immediately draw 1 card and replace itself.\n* {{C|Rupture}} can be used to also provide the player with additional {{BD|Strength}} whenever they damage themselves to trigger {{R|Runic Cube}}.\n* Intentionally taking damage from small multi-attacks (such as from a {{M|Byrd|Byrd's}} {{Int|Byrd|Peck}}, or from any enemy while having {{BD|Intangible}} to reduce all damage to 1) can provide a lot of card draw for the player's next turn.\n* Finding alternative methods of healing beyond just {{R|Burning Blood}} can help the player take advantage of {{R|Runic Cube}} more safely, such as {{C|Reaper}}, {{R|Meat on the Bone}}, or {{P|Blood Potion}}.\n* Intentionally attacking into an enemy with {{BD|Thorns}} or {{BD|Sharp Hide}} can be used to lose HP and trigger {{R|Runic Cube}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Tungsten Rod}} can either help or hinder making effective use of {{R|Runic Cube}} depending on the scenario. For example:\n** It can prevent 1 copy of {{C|Combust}} from drawing and immediately discarding a card. This effectively prevents {{C|Combust}} from wasting a card draw.\n** Prevents {{R|Blue Candle}} from drawing a card, and fully disables the \"infinite\" card draw combo of repeatedly {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausting}} {{C|Necronomicurse}} to intentionally take 1 damage and trigger {{R|Runic Cube}}.\n** Prevents 1 copy of {{C|Brutality}} from drawing 1 additional card at the start of the player's turn. This can be somewhat circumvented by playing multiple copies of {{C|Brutality}}. For example, if 2 {{C|Brutality}} are in play then their damage will stack into a single instance of 2 damage, will be reduced to 1 damage by {{R|Tungsten Rod}}, and trigger {{R|Runic Cube}} as desired.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{BD|Buffer}} (through {{R|Fossilized Helix}} or {{C|Buffer}}) can make it more difficult to trigger {{R|Runic Cube}}'s card draw as any HP loss is prevented.\n* Since {{C|Combust}}, {{C|Regret}}, and the {{BD|Constricted}} debuff from {{M|Spire Growth}} all deal damage ''after'' the player's turn has ended but ''before'' their hand is discarded, any cards drawn from those effects will be immediately discarded and give the player no opportunity to use them.\n* {{R|Mark of the Bloom}} prevents all forms of healing, which can make intentionally taking damage to trigger {{R|Runic Cube}} significantly more dangerous.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The runes in {{R|Runic Cube}} can be seen on the left side of {{R|Stone Calendar}}.\n* {{R|Runic Cube}} is part of the \"runic relic set\" alongside {{R|Runic Capacitor}}, {{R|Runic Dome}}, and {{R|Runic Pyramid}}. Unlike most other \"relic sets\", the runic relics have no common mechanic and are purely related by name and visual design.\n** {{R|Runic Cube}} is the only {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Ironclad|Ironclad-exclusive}} runic relic.\n\n== Update History ==\n* Weekly Patch 11: A Library of Features\n** Runic Cube relic buffed. No longer has a drawback (used to reduce your Max HP on obtain)\n* Weekly Patch 6: We're Back!\n** Runic Cube now triggers off of Thorns as intended.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3125":{"pageid":3125,"ns":0,"title":"Ssserpent Head","revisions":[{"revid":51010,"parentid":50999,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Ssserpent Head\n|Image       = SsserpentHead.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = Face Trader\n|Description = Whenever you enter a ? room, gain 50 [[Gold]].\n|Flavor      = The most fulfilling of lives is that in which you can buy anything!\n}}\n{{R|Ssserpent Head}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained from {{E|Face Trader}} by selecting the {{Frame|Trade}} option and randomly receiving {{R|Ssserpent Head}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Combined with the {{R|Bloody Idol}}, the player will restore 5 HP whenever they enter a [[Map Locations#Unknown|? Room]].\n* The purchasing power (or \"effective gold\") at [[Merchant|Shops]] granted by {{R|Ssserpent Head}} increases if the player has {{R|The Courier}} and/or {{R|Membership Card}}.\n<!--\nBe very careful with the math if you decide rewrite this section.\nThe correct formula for purchasing power (or \"effective gold\") is [  50 / (1 - discount)  ] and NOT [  50 * (1 + discount)  ].\nA 50% discount should DOUBLE the purchasing power (+100%)! It's not just a 50% increase.\n-->\n** {{R|The Courier|The Courier's}} 20% discount makes the 50 [[Gold]] from {{R|Ssserpent Head}} have the purchasing power of 62.5 [[Gold]].\n** {{R|Membership Card|Membership Card's}} 50% discount makes the 50 [[Gold]] from {{R|Ssserpent Head}} have the purchasing power of 100 [[Gold]].\n** Having both {{R|The Courier}} and {{R|Membership Card}} is a total 60% discount. This makes the 50 [[Gold]] from {{R|Ssserpent Head}} have the purchasing power of 125 [[Gold]].\n* {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} may help the player path into more [[Map Locations#Unknown|? Rooms]] by ensuring they will not contain combat.\n* {{R|Tiny Chest}} may also incentivize the player to path into more [[Map Locations#Unknown|? Rooms]] and can reward them with more [[Map Locations#Treasure Room|Treasure Rooms]].\n* {{R|Wing Boots}} may help the player path into more [[Map Locations#Unknown|? Rooms]].\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Ssserpent Head}} is the face of {{E|The Ssssserpent}} that was taken by the {{E|Face Trader}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** Face Trader event will no longer give out duplicate relics on Endless mode.\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Adding event relic Ssserpent Head.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2642":{"pageid":2642,"ns":0,"title":"Sundial","revisions":[{"revid":55965,"parentid":50859,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Sundial\n|Image       = Sundial.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Every 3 times you shuffle your draw pile, gain 2 {{KW|Energy}}.\n|Flavor      = \"Early man's foolish obsession with time caused them to look to the sky for guidance, hoping for something permanent.\" - Zoroth\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{R|Sundial}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]]. The counter that keeps track of how many times the draw pile has been shuffled is not reset between combats.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Sundial}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Sundial}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Sundial}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Sundial}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Sundial}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Sundial}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Sundial}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Sundial}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Sundial}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Sundial}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Sundial}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Sundial}} triggers its {{KW|Energy}} gain ''after'' the card draw effect has finished resolving.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|The Abacus}} grants {{KW|Block}} every time the draw pile is shuffled; i.e. a total of 18 {{KW|Block}} per trigger of {{R|Sundial}}.\n* {{R|Melange}} will {{KW|Scry}} 3 cards every time the draw pile is shuffled. This can be particularly useful when it coincides with {{R|Sundial}} triggering its {{KW|Energy}} gain as the {{KW|Scry}} can help ensure more desirable cards are drawn to spend the extra energy on.\n* {{C|Reboot}} and {{C|Deep Breath}} will each add +1 to {{R|Sundial}}'s counter as long as there is at least 1 card in the player's discard pile.\n* Small decks, either achieved from card removes (that may be aided by {{R|Smiling Mask}}, {{R|Membership Card}}, or {{R|Peace Pipe}}) or {{KW|Exhaust}} cards (such as {{C|Fiend Fire}} or {{C|Burning Pact}}), will have an easier time triggering {{R|Sundial}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n<!-- This section is included in every Relic that triggers \"whenever you shuffle your draw pile\". If you make any changes to this list, please update other relics like The Abacus and Melange! -->\n* Effects that say \"''Shuffle a card into your draw pile.''\" do not actually shuffle the draw pile, instead inserting a card into the draw pile in a random position. Therefore, these effects do '''not''' trigger {{R|Sundial}}:\n** {{C|Wild Strike}}\n** {{C|Reckless Charge}}\n** {{C|Evaluate}}\n** {{C|Pray}}\n** {{C|Reach Heaven}}\n** {{C|Study}}\n** {{C|Tantrum}}\n** {{C|Alpha}}\n** {{C|Conjure Blade}}\n** {{C|Chrysalis}}\n** {{C|Metamorphosis}}\n** {{C|Beta}}\n** {{R|Nilry's Codex}}\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Velvet Choker}} will effectively disable infinite combos that rely on {{R|Sundial}}.\n\n== Bugs ==\n* If the player has an empty draw pile, exactly 1 card in their discard pile, and then uses an effect that draws 2 or more cards (e.g. {{C|Wheel Kick}} or {{P|Swift Potion}}), the draw pile will be shuffled ''twice'' (therefore adding +2 to {{R|Sundial}}'s counter instead of the expected +1).\n** This bug enables a whole suite of infinite combos that rely on {{R|Sundial}}, a very small deck, and any combination of exactly 2 cards which draw at least 3 cards between themselves with both cards drawing at least 1 card. The simplest such combo is an {{KW|Upgrade|upgraded}} {{C|Pommel Strike}}+ and a {{C|Shrug It Off}}; the player will be able to infinitely play those two cards one after the other and remain {{KW|Energy}} neutral while slowly dealing damage with the {{C|Pommel Strike}}+. This combo in particular is also enough to fully block {{M|Corrupt Heart}}'s {{BD|Beat of Death}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A sundial is a device that can be used to tell the time of day when sunlight shines on it.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sundial</ref>\n** Sundials have been used to tell the time for thousands of years (first around 1500 BC; roughly 3500 years ago) and were used to calculate the circumference of Earth by the Greek mathematician [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eratosthenes Eratosthenes] around 240 BC.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eratosthenes#Measurement_of_Earth's_circumference</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Updated relic asset for Sundial.\n* '''Weekly Patch 47: Metamorphosis'''\n** Reboot and Deep Breath cards no longer trigger shuffle-based relics twice.\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Sundial relic wording improvements.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3129":{"pageid":3129,"ns":0,"title":"Thread and Needle","revisions":[{"revid":55977,"parentid":55215,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Rename|Thread and Needle|Gorget}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Thread and Needle\n|Image       = ThreadandNeedle.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = At the start of each combat, gain 4 {{BD|Plated Armor}}.\n|Flavor      = Wrapping the magical thread around your body, you feel harder to the touch.\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Plated Armor}}\n{{R|Thread and Needle}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Thread and Needle}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Thread and Needle}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Thread and Needle}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Thread and Needle}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Thread and Needle}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Thread and Needle}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Thread and Needle}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Thread and Needle}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Thread and Needle}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Thread and Needle}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Thread and Needle}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Thread and Needle}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{BD|Plated Armor}} will trigger ''after'' {{R|Orichalcum}} has checked whether the player has 0 Icon {{KW|Block}}, allowing both {{R|Thread and Needle}} and {{R|Orichalcum}} to be used together effectively.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{BD|Plated Armor}} can be used with other end-of-turn {{KW|Block}} effects (such as {{C|Metallicize}}, {{R|Orichalcum}}, and {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}}) to gain a fair amount of passive {{KW|Block}} without needing to draw or play as many defensive cards each turn.\n* {{BD|Plated Armor}} can still provide {{KW|Block}} even if the player is affected by the {{BD|No Block}} effect from {{C|Panic Button}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{BD|Plated Armor}} is not lost from self-damage effects like {{C|Combust}} or {{C|Bloodletting}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 37: Darkness'''\n** Thread and Needle relic nerf: 5 -> 4 Plated Armor.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2564":{"pageid":2564,"ns":0,"title":"Toxic Egg","revisions":[{"revid":55983,"parentid":55516,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Toxic Egg\n|Image       = ToxicEgg2.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Whenever you add a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill}} card to your deck, {{KW|Upgrade}} it.\n|Flavor      = \"What a marvelous discovery! This appears to be the inert egg of some magical creature. Who or what created this?\" - Ranwid\n|Keywords    = Deck\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Upgrade}}{{R|Toxic Egg}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Toxic Egg}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Toxic Egg}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Toxic Egg}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Toxic Egg}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Toxic Egg}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Toxic Egg}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Toxic Egg}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Toxic Egg}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Toxic Egg}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Toxic Egg}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Toxic Egg}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Prayer Wheel}} and {{R|Question Card}} can provide more opportunities for the player to encounter {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}} from card rewards.\n* {{R|Toxic Egg}} will automatically upgrade all {{C|Apparition|Apparitions}} from the {{E|Council of Ghosts}} event.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}} created mid-combat (such as from {{R|Nilry's Codex}} which adds to the draw pile, or {{C|Distraction}} which adds to the hand) will not be {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} as {{R|Toxic Egg}} only '''Upgrades''' card that are added to the player's '''deck'''.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* In some niche scenarios, the player may prefer if a certain {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill}} were ''not'' upgraded, and {{R|Toxic Egg}} does not give the player a choice on whether the cards be '''Upgraded''' or not. Some examples include:\n** Preferring a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill}} were ''not'' made {{KW|Innate}} from its {{KW|Upgrade}} (such as {{C|Chill}} or {{C|Alpha}}).\n** Preferring to only discard 1 card from {{C|Prepared}} (instead of 2 cards from {{C|Prepared}}+).\n** Preferring to discard all 3 cards from {{C|Concentrate}} (instead of only 2 cards from {{C|Concentrate}}+).\n** Preferring to only lose 1 {{BD|Focus}} from {{C|Reprogram}} (instead of 2 focus from {{C|Reprogram}}+).\n** Preferring to only {{KW|Channel}} 1 Orb from {{C|Chaos}} (instead of 2 Orbs from {{C|Chaos}}+).\n** Preferring to only {{KW|Retain}} 1 card from {{C|Meditate}} (instead of 2 cards from {{C|Meditate}}+).\n** If the player has {{R|Snecko Eye}} and a way to upgrade cards mid-combat (such as {{C|Armaments}}), they will not be able to take advantage of reducing a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill's}} {{KW|Energy}} cost if {{R|Snecko Eye}} makes it more expensive than its base cost.\n*** For example, if {{R|Snecko Eye}} makes an un-{{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} {{C|Seeing Red}} cost 2 or 3 {{KW|Energy}}, '''Upgrading''' it will set its cost to 0 (the normal cost of {{C|Seeing Red}}+). But if it's already '''Upgraded''' in the player's deck from {{R|Toxic Egg}}, this trick cannot be performed.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Toxic Egg}} is one of the relics in the \"egg relic set\" which {{KW|Upgrade}} cards of different types when added to the player's deck; the others being {{R|Molten Egg}} for {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} and {{R|Frozen Egg}} for {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}}.\n* {{R|Toxic Egg}} is called <code>Toxic_Egg_2</code> internally as the egg relics were reworked in '''Weekly Patch 10: Quality of Life'''. These reworked versions ended up being the final version of the egg relics, so their modified internal name became their default internal name.\n* The visual design of {{R|Toxic Egg}} is a reference to the egg portrayed in the iconic poster for the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_(film) 1979 film ''Alien''].\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Egg relics now update card rewards in reward screens upon pickup.\n** Egg relics update card previews on Pandora's Box confirmation screen.\n** Fixed egg relics not upgrading card received from A Note To Yourself.\n* '''Weekly Patch 53: Blizzard'''\n** Fix for Egg relics causing an issue with cards in the Library event screen.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Egg relics no longer spawn beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** Gremlin Match and Keep event now has cards upgraded correctly.\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Buying an egg relic in the shop will now correctly show cards in the shop upgraded.\n* '''Weekly Patch 31: Custom Mode'''\n** Match and Keep cards are now viewed as upgraded w/ Egg relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 27: Hello World'''\n** Egg relics now show pre-upgraded cards for Sensory Stone event's choices.\n* '''Weekly Patch 24: Potions?!'''\n** The three egg relics will now show the cards they are affecting as upgraded in card reward screens and shops.\n* '''Weekly Patch 13: Thinking Ahead'''\n** Egg relics (Toxic, Molten, Toxic) can now appear at Neow and some other scenarios.\n* '''Weekly Patch 10: Quality of Life'''\n** All 3 Egg relics (Molten/Toxic/Toxic) are now reworked. New eggs are Uncommon.\n** Toxic Egg: Whenever you add a Skill card to your deck, it is Upgraded.\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** You can no longer cancel out of the selection screen for Egg relics.\n** Toxic Egg is now Common (was Uncommon)\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2540":{"pageid":2540,"ns":0,"title":"Vajra","revisions":[{"revid":55992,"parentid":50829,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Vajra\n|Image       = Vajra.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = At the start of each combat, gain 1 {{BD|Strength}}.\n|Flavor      = An ornamental relic given to warriors displaying glory in battle.\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Strength}}{{R|Vajra}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Vajra}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Vajra}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Vajra}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Vajra}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Vajra}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Vajra}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Vajra}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Vajra}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Vajra}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Vajra}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\nWhen calculating damage dealt, all the additive bonuses are added first, and then multiplicative bonuses are multiplied. The final result is rounded down. {{R|Vajra}}'s {{BD|Strength}} is an additive bonus.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{BD|Strength}} is applied to each hit in an {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}}, allowing multi-hit '''attacks''' (such as {{C|Sword Boomerang}}, {{C|Flechettes}}, {{C|Barrage}}, and {{C|Bowling Bash}}) to benefit from '''strength''' multiple times.\n* {{C|Heavy Blade}} specifically benefits from {{BD|Strength}} multiple times in its effect, even though it's a single-hit {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}}.\n* {{C|Reaper}}'s healing increases based on its unblocked damage, and therefore more {{BD|Strength}} will cause it to heal more HP.\n* {{C|Wallop}}'s {{KW|Block}} gain increases based on its unblocked damage, and therefore more {{BD|Strength}} will cause it to gain more '''block'''.\n* {{C|Limit Break}} can be used to multiply the player's current {{BD|Strength}}, although it won't be a very significant increase if their only '''strength''' source is {{R|Vajra}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A vajra is a legendary and ritualistic tool from some East-Asian religious cultures, namely Buddhism, Hinduism, and Jainism.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vajra</ref> It is also occasionally described as a \"ritual weapon\".\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 23: Testing Continues...'''\n** Wording improvements for Vajra and Oddly Smooth Stone relic.\n* '''Weekly Patch 10: Quality of Life'''\n** Vajra flavor text typo fix.\n* '''Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!'''\n** Vajra gets updated art.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]}}}}